AB  - Student dropout remains a persistent challenge in higher education, particularly within engineering and technology undergraduate programs. Although several machine learning approaches achieve high predictive accuracy, their inherent lack of interpretability often constrains pedagogical teams from designing and justifying evidence-based interventions. To address this issue, this paper presents a model that integrates Explainable Boosting Machines (EBM) for cross-sectional risk scoring with Gaussian Hidden Markov Models (G-HMM) for temporal trajectory modeling. The EBM component produces inherently interpretable shape functions to quantify nonlinear relationships between academic indicators and dropout risk. Complementarily, the G-HMM captures transition dynamics by modeling the temporal evolution of these scores across five latent states. The experimental evaluation was conducted using a 15-year dataset from an electrical engineering program. Starting from an initial set of 71 academic indicators, a multi-phase selection algorithm identifies the most discriminative features for each program, ensuring model parsimony and performance. Based on these selected features, the EBM achieved an F1-score of 97.81% with 100% sensitivity. The G-HMM complemented this result by modeling temporal trajectories across five latent states, achieving a mean detection lead time of 2.60 semesters prior to dropout. Unlike traditional baselines, the proposed methodology generates interpretable early-warning signals that facilitate the design and monitoring of targeted interventions. This approach enables institutions to quantify the impact of corrective actions across successive academic cycles.  © 2013 IEEE.
